v 2.9.3, October 2026 - outputs overwritten in place, safe in synchronized folders (Proton Drive, iCloud, OneDrive); failed workflow steps keep their outputs:
- outputs of a former run that a script writes again are overwritten in place, and those it does not write again are removed at the end of the run (formerly all were removed first): in folders synchronized by Proton Drive, iCloud or OneDrive, deleting a file and writing it again at once made "name 2" copies and lost the originals; when a script stops on an error, the outputs of its former run are kept
- umd_workflow.py: a step that fails keeps the files of its former run (formerly removed) and runs again the next time
- umd2out.py: writes the DeepMD sets into the existing set folders

v 2.9.4, October 2026 - LAMMPS parser checked on real runs; files without forces, stresses or velocities handled:
- LAMMPSParser2umd.py checked on a real LAMMPS run (DeePMD water + NaCl, NPT): KineticEnergyIons = TotEng - PotEng when the thermo output has no KinEng; the pair_coeff command is found also when the deepmd pair style prints it on the line of its info
- files without forces or stresses (e.g. LAMMPS dumps without fx fy fz, logs without Pxx...): check_umd.py skips the net-force and stress checks, viscosity_umd.py stops with an explanation (it gave 0), umd2out.py writes no force.npy in the DeepMD sets
- LAMMPSParser2umd.py checked on a second set (pyrolite melt, dump without id, velocities or forces, with a stress file -a): it reports the steps of the stress file and warns when frames have no line there (their stress was 0 silently), notes that a dump without id must keep the order of the atoms, and says when the dump has no velocities; check_umd.py skips the jump, time-step and temperature checks without velocities, and the energy drift and equilibration of fields 0 in every snapshot

v 2.10, November 2026 - ABINIT parser; a void in build_umd.py; LAMMPS NPT runs unwrapped in reduced coordinates:
- AbinitParser.py (new): ABINIT molecular dynamics output -> UMD, in the layout of ABINIT 6.4.1 (checked on an ionmov 9 run, spin polarized, non-orthogonal cell); other values of ionmov are flagged, variable cells and several datasets are refused
- check_umd.py: the smearing check is skipped when ElectronicEntropy is 0 in every snapshot (it reported OK)
- build_umd.py: --vacuum adds a void along x, y, z (the vectors a, b, c), independently, each with its own thickness, which the script asks for when it is not on the command line
- LAMMPSParser2umd.py checked on two more real runs, with velocities, forces and full thermo output. With a changing box (NPT) the unwrapped positions no longer follow xu, in which an atom at rest moves with the box, more the more cells it crossed; parse NPT runs again. Masses from the log also for dumps without a type column
- check_umd.py: the unwrapping check accepts motion in reduced coordinates when the cell changes, the jump check allows for the move with the cell, the energy drift says when EnergyWithDrift is only the total energy (no econserve in the LAMMPS thermo output)

v 2.11, November 2026 - the graphical interface set aside:
- The graphical interface (UMD_GUI.py, averages_forGUI.py, val_extract_umd.py) is set aside in the gui folder: it no longer ran (it imported scripts replaced during the cleanup) and is not maintained

v 2.12, November 2026 - a validation suite; two corrections found by it:
- validation/ (new): a validation suite. run_validation.py runs every script, with its main options, on the reference runs of data/ (VASP, ABINIT and LAMMPS outputs parsed; the workflow on glycine, pyrolite, ZnS, corundum, hydrous andesite and on small odd files; builds, conversions, speciation, transport and thermodynamics options) and on synthetic files with exact answers (ideal gas, fcc in two cells, rigid water, random walks with a D tensor, Ornstein-Uhlenbeck velocities, harmonic oscillators, Ornstein-Uhlenbeck stresses, harmonic wells for thermodynamic integration, injected faults). It compares key numbers and checksums of every output with the references of validation/reference/, and the synthetic results with their exact values
- averages.py -a: a UMD file without Pressure, Temperature or InternalEnergy (positions only) is skipped with a message; it stopped the run (a traceback, or no fullthermo.dat for the other files)
- vdos_thermo.py: the warning on negative parts of the vDOS advises smoothing or a longer window when the Hann taper was already used

v 2.13, November 2026 - faster speciation angles, bond lifetimes, averages and reading of the atom lines:
- speciation_and_angles.py -t 1: the angles of a snapshot are computed in one pass (formerly one central atom at a time) and written in one block, and the angle file has the angles with 2 decimals (formerly 17 digits; the means keep all their digits): 4.5 times faster (ZnS 72 s to 16 s), the angle file 40 % smaller; the other outputs identical
- speciation_and_angles.py -l 1: the bond lifetimes are computed in numpy (ZnS 2.5 s to 0.7 s); output identical
- check_umd.py, umd2out.py: the atom lines are read by numpy's text parser (np.loadtxt), 1.7 times faster; outputs identical
- averages.py: the fields are read in one pass over the file in Python (formerly one grep per field, and grep is not on Windows): -a 4 times faster (hydrous andesite 10.3 s to 2.8 s); outputs identical

v 2.14, November 2026 - snapshots in batches sized from their bytes, read by the workers:
- Snapshots are given to the parallel workers in batches sized from the bytes of a snapshot in the file (many snapshots per task for small systems, one for big ones), at most two batches per worker at a time, and the workers read their own snapshots from the file (an index of the byte offset of every snapshot, files above 2 GB included): the memory holds a few batches whatever the length of the run, and the results are written as they come
- bonding_umd.py: the bonds of a snapshot are sorted and written in C, by the workers: hydrous andesite 8.8 s to 1.3 s, memory 770 MB to 77 MB; output identical
- speciation_and_angles.py: the clusters, angles, the step file and the bond lifetimes are computed by batches in the workers: ZnS -r 1 -t 1 -l 1 16 s to 6.8 s, hydrous andesite -r 0 5.3 s to 2.4 s; outputs identical
- gofr_umd.py: processes instead of threads (the C reader spent its time on a system lock in threads), each reading its block from the file: hydrous andesite 1.65 s to 1.0 s, memory 307 MB to 65 MB; output identical for the same -k

v 3.0, November 2026 - stable production version: the package cleaned, corrected and checked script by script (2.7 to 2.14), the graphical interface set aside, a validation suite, parallel processing on CPUs:
- every script rewritten or checked for any cell shape and for NPT runs, with errors on the statistical results (see 2.7 to 2.14)
- the graphical interface is set aside in the gui folder (2.11)
- validation/: every script checked on reference runs and on synthetic runs with exact answers (2.12)
- parallel processing on CPUs: snapshots given to worker processes in batches sized from their bytes, read by the workers from the file (2.14)
- ROADMAP: the plan of the v3.Y versions, towards systems of up to 1 billion atoms

v 3.0.1, November 2026 - CP2K parser:
- CP2KParser.py (new): CP2K molecular dynamics run -> UMD. CP2K writes a run in several files, matched here by their MD step: positions (<project>-pos-1.xyz, XMOL or extended XYZ), and when given velocities (-v), forces (-r), energies (-e, the .ener file), stress tensor (-a, the .stress file: total pressure tensor, kinetic part included, as the UMD StressTensor) and cell. The xyz files of CP2K have no cell: -c takes numbers, the .cell file (the cell of every step, NPT), an input or restart file (&CELL section, and the MASS of the &KIND sections), or the main output. With the standard names only -f is needed. Masses other than the standard ones (deuterium) with -m: the parser compares the kinetic energy of the velocities with that of the energy file and prints the masses that fit when they differ. A run restarted from an earlier step, appended to the same files: of the steps written several times the last ones are kept. Unwrapped positions in reduced coordinates for a changing cell, as LAMMPSParser2umd.py. Checked on one real run (100 H2O + 2 H2SO4, NVT, 1001 steps, CP2K 2023.2; cell from the MD_INI lines of its main output): every value of the UMD file equals the CP2K files to its rounding; the cell file, the input, output and extended XYZ layouts, and NPT were checked on files written for the tests, not yet on real ones

Since v 3.0.1 (not yet released):
- rotations_umd.py (new): orientations of the molecular units of a run, a central atom with its bonded neighbours from the bonding file (-c O -a H water, -c N -a H ammonia, -c C -a H methane; -x the number of neighbours, default the most frequent composition; a snapshot counts only with exactly this composition). Orientation by the dipole axis (the sum of the bond vectors: H2O, NH3, OH) or, for units without a dipole (CH4, SO4, CO3), by the bonds, chosen automatically (-u). The correlation of order l between two units is the mean of P_l between their vectors: for dipoles the cosine of their angle (l = 1) and P2; for bonds the orders that the shape of the unit allows (a tetrahedron: 3 and 4). Outputs per unit: correlations between two units against their distance, with their g(r), the dipolar term and the Kirkwood factor (.rot.pair.dat); distribution of the angle between the dipoles of first neighbours, of all and of the linked ones, where a bond of one unit points to the other, i.e. the hydrogen bond (.rot.shell.dat); correlations in time of the orientation of a unit, of its bonds followed atom by atom, and of the total orientation of all the units (.rot.time.dat); map of the orientations on the sphere and its entropy relative to free rotation, with a check of its sampling (.rot.map.dat); summary (.rot.dat). A unit that loses its composition for less than -g fs (default 50) goes on as the same molecule; -m minimum life. Any cell, NPT; errors from blocks. Checked on synthetic runs with exact answers (rotational diffusion of H2O and CH4, pairs with parallel, antiparallel, identical and inverted orientations, jumps between six directions) and on ice VII, liquid water, glycine and a hydrous melt. Entropy of the units by the two-phase thermodynamic model (2PT; Lin, Maiti, Goddard 2010), when the UMD file has velocities: densities of states of the translation of the centre of mass, of the rotation (angular velocities in a body frame followed by fitting the shape of the unit at the start of its life, weighted by the moments of inertia; for linear units in the frame of the cell) and of the internal motions; translation and rotation each with its fluidicity, split into gas-like parts (hard spheres; rigid rotor, with the symmetry number found from the shapes of the units, -y to set it) and solid-like parts (quantum oscillators) (.rot.2pt.dat; entropies in kB and J/(mol K) with block errors in .rot.dat, with the kinetic temperatures, moments of inertia, entropy of the free rotor and the change for 10 % more volume). Volume of a unit (-V): a number, Bader volumes per element (-V O:16.5,H:4.2), or the ACF.dat files of the Bader program for snapshots of the run, matched to their snapshot (its number in the name of the file or of its folder, counted from 0 as umd2out.py) by the positions of the atoms in any order, A or bohr, any origin, so that the Bader analysis can come from any code; default the cell volume per unit, with a warning when other atoms are present. -t temperature. Checked on synthetic rigid rotors (H2O, CH4, OH) with Ornstein-Uhlenbeck translation and rotation: fluidicities equal to the exact ones to 1e-8, entropies to 1e-3, symmetry numbers 2, 12, 1, Bader volumes exact; ice VII (f 0.002 and 0.03) and liquid water (f 0.30 and 0.05)
